  "illegible": [
    "Comté card (first row, fourth column), style line: the plate prints 'Bold, tarity, blue', and 'tarity' is not a word; it is most likely a misprint of 'tangy' (the Roquefort card beside it prints 'Bold, tangy, blue'). Transcribed as printed."
  ],
  "corrections": [
    {
      "on": "Comté",
      "says": "Bold, tarity, blue",
      "should": "Comté is a firm, nutty, aged Alpine cheese with no blue veining, so the style line should read something like nutty, firm, complex.",
      "why": "Comté is a pressed cooked hard cheese, the plate's own Cheese Categories panel files it under Hard cheeses, the wedge pictured is a plain firm cheese, and the line simply repeats the Roquefort card with tangy misprinted as tarity."
    },
    {
      "on": "Époisses",
      "says": "Smoky, rich, pungent",
      "should": "Époisses is a washed rind cheese and is never smoked, so smoky should be replaced with a word like meaty or salty.",
      "why": "Époisses de Bourgogne is washed with brine and marc de Bourgogne, and the app's own deck describes the washed rind family as brine or booze washed and stinky, never smoked."
    },
    {
      "on": "Parmigiano Reggiano",
      "says": "Pictured as a bright orange crumbly block",
      "should": "Parmigiano Reggiano has a pale straw yellow paste with a crystalline grain, and the orange block pictured looks like colored cheddar.",
      "why": "Parmigiano Reggiano is never colored with annatto, so its paste is straw colored and the picture would mislead a server at the counter."
    },
    {
      "on": "Blue Shropshire",
      "says": "Blue Shropshire, pictured with a white paste",
      "should": "The cheese is called Shropshire Blue, and its paste is orange from annatto with blue veins rather than the white paste shown.",
      "why": "Shropshire Blue is the name used by every maker, and it is an annatto tinted Stilton style blue, so both the reversed name and the white paste picture would teach a server to misname and misidentify it."
    }
  ]
